.TH CLRUCACHE_SIZE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_size \- return the number of live entries in the cache
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

size_t clrucache_size(clru_cache cache);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of live entries that the cache holds now. A live
entry is one that is fully initialised, and that no eviction removed.
The count leaves out a placeholder entry that a fetch or a set in
progress created.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache  Cache handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The number of live entries. It is 0 when cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID,
and 0 when cache is a stale handle that a call already destroyed.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle. The library resolves it through a slot
table that the library owns, before it touches the cache object behind
the handle. A stale handle here is an ordinary error, and not a fatal
one. Only clru_destroy() treats a stale handle as fatal.
This function adds the sizes of the segments of the cache. It takes the
own lock of each segment in turn. The result is a snapshot, and not a
value at one instant. A segment can gain or lose an entry after this
function counts it. The total therefore describes no single moment.
The complexity is O(number of segments). That is a small fixed bound.
It is not O(entries).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clrucache_capacity (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
